Wh.\\n\\n---\\n\\nTOPIC 7 (Tier 3, asked 2/9): ECBC CODE\\n\\nQ: Define ECBC and its significance in promoting energy-efficient building construction. (7 marks)\\n\\nAnswer should cover:\\n- Full form: Energy Conservation Building Code\\n- Launched by BEE in 2007 (updated 2017 as ECBC 2017)\\n- Applies to commercial buildings with connected load of 100 kW or more, or contract demand of 120 kVA or more\\n- Covers 6 building systems: building envelope, HVAC, lighting, electrical systems, water heating, renewable energy\\n- Sets minimum energy performance standards for new commercial buildings\\n- Three levels of compliance: ECBC (baseline), ECBC+ (enhanced), SuperECBC (near net-zero)\\n- ECBC buildings consume 25-50% less energy than conventional buildings\\n- States can adopt and notify ECBC with modifications for local conditions\\n- Currently voluntary, but some states (Andhra Pradesh, Telangana, UP, Rajasthan) have made it mandatory\\n\\n---\\n\\nTOPIC 8 (Tier 3, asked 1/9 but explicitly in syllabus): ELECTRICITY ACT 2003\\n\\nQ: Discuss the provisions of Electricity Act 2003 related to energy conservation. (7 marks)\\n\\nAnswer:\\n- Enacted in 2003, replaced Indian Electricity Act 1910, Electricity Supply Act 1948, and Electricity Regulatory Commissions Act 1998\\n- Section 3: National electricity policy and plan must include provisions for energy conservation\\n- Section 61: Tariff regulations to promote efficiency in electricity use\\n- Section 86(1)(e): State commissions to promote cogeneration and renewable sources\\n- Section 166: State governments to promote energy efficiency\\n- De-licensing of power generation (except hydro above 25 MW and nuclear)\\n- Open access provisions to promote competition and reduce transmission losses\\n- Impact on energy security: increased private participation, reduced T&D losses, promoted competition\\n\\n---\\n\\nTOPIC 9 (Tier 3, asked 1/9 but in syllabus): NATIONAL ACTION PLAN ON CLIMATE CHANGE\\n\\nQ: List the missions under the National Action Plan on Climate Change (NAPCC). (3 marks)\\n\\nAnswer:\\nNAPCC was launched by India in June 2008. It has 8 National Missions:\\n1. National Solar Mission\\n2. National Mission for Enhanced Energy Efficiency (NMEEE)\\n3. National Mission on Sustainable Habitat\\n4. National Water Mission\\n5. National Mission for Sustaining the Himalayan Ecosystem\\n6. National Mission for a Green India\\n7. National Mission for Sustainable Agriculture\\n8. National Mission on Strategic Knowledge for Climate Change\\n\\n---\\n\\nTOPIC 10 (Tier 3, asked 1/9): ENERGY PRICING\\n\\nQ: Explain in brief energy pricing. (3 marks)\\n\\nEnergy pricing is the mechanism by which energy costs are determined for consumers. In India, energy prices are set through a mix of market forces and government regulation.\\n- Electricity tariffs are set by State Electricity Regulatory Commissions (SERCs) and vary by consumer category (domestic, commercial, industrial, agricultural)\\n- Petroleum product prices (petrol, diesel) were deregulated in 2010-2014 and now follow international crude oil prices\\n- Natural gas prices are set by the government every 6 months using a formula linked to international gas prices\\n- Coal prices are set by Coal India Ltd with government oversight\\n- Cross-subsidization: industrial and commercial consumers pay higher rates to subsidize do\n---\nThe checkpoint summary has been truncated due to hitting max length. Please see the conversation log file for the full conversation."}
